quickAnswer: "Type a password to see a strength score from 0 (very weak) to 4 (very strong), how long an attacker would need to guess it, and what makes it weak. The check uses zxcvbn-ts, which looks for common passwords, words, names, keyboard patterns, dates and substitutions like 0 for o, and runs entirely on your device. Long, unique passphrases score best; 'password' scores 0."
formula:
  title: "How Strength Is Estimated"
  expression: "Score = f(log10 guesses)   ·   guesses = the fewest guesses an attacker needs, using dictionaries, patterns and brute force"
  explanation: "zxcvbn splits the password into pieces it recognises: dictionary words and common passwords (also when misspelled or written with substitutions like 4 for a), names, dates, repeats, sequences such as abc or 123, and keyboard walks such as qwerty. It finds the cheapest combination of those pieces and counts how many guesses a cracker following that strategy would need. Fewer than 1,000 guesses scores 0, fewer than a million 1, fewer than 100 million 2, fewer than 10 billion 3, and anything more 4. Crack times divide the guesses by attack speeds: 100 guesses per hour for an online login with rate limiting, and 10 billion per second for an offline attack on a fast, unsalted hash."
