faqs:
  - q: "How strong is my password?"
    a: "Type it into the box. You will see a score from 0 to 4, estimated crack times for online and offline attacks, and specific warnings, such as 'this is a common password' or 'dates are easy to guess'."
  - q: "Is it safe to type my password here?"
    a: "The check runs entirely in your browser, in a background worker on this page. The password is not sent, stored, logged or put in the page address, and the field is cleared when you leave. If you prefer, test a password that is similar in style instead of your real one."
  - q: "What makes a password strong?"
    a: "Length and unpredictability. A random password of 15 or more characters, or a passphrase of several unrelated words, is strong. Common words, names, dates, keyboard patterns and simple substitutions make it weak."
  - q: "How long should a password be?"
    a: "NIST SP 800-63B-4 requires at least 15 characters for passwords used on their own and at least 8 when combined with another factor, and recommends allowing at least 64 characters. Longer is better."
  - q: "Are password complexity rules still recommended?"
    a: "No. NIST says services should not require mixtures of character types, and should instead block common and compromised passwords and allow long passphrases."
  - q: "What is zxcvbn?"
    a: "An open-source password strength estimator first released by Dropbox. This tool uses zxcvbn-ts, a maintained TypeScript version, with its English and common-password dictionaries hosted on this site."
  - q: "How is the crack time calculated?"
    a: "The estimated number of guesses is divided by an attack speed: 100 guesses per hour for a rate-limited online login and 10 billion per second for an offline attack on a fast hash. Real attacks vary, so treat the times as a guide."
  - q: "How do I make a strong password I can remember?"
    a: "Use a passphrase of four or more random, unrelated words, or let a password manager or the ToolsBuilt password generator create and store a random password for you."
