    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>What NIST Says About Passwords, in Plain Words</h2>
      <p>The US National Institute of Standards and Technology publishes the guidance many organisations follow, SP 800-63B. Its current revision (SP 800-63B-4) turned years of research into a few clear rules for services that set password policies:</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Length over complexity:</strong> a password used on its own must be at least 15 characters (at least 8 if it is one part of multi-factor login), and services should allow at least 64 characters.</li>
        <li><strong>No composition rules:</strong> services should not force mixtures like "one upper case, one number, one symbol"; those rules produce predictable passwords such as Password1!.</li>
        <li><strong>Block bad passwords:</strong> new passwords must be checked against a list of common, expected and previously breached passwords, and rejected if they appear.</li>
        <li><strong>No forced periodic changes:</strong> services should not make people change passwords on a schedule, but must force a change when there is evidence a password has been compromised.</li>
        <li><strong>Allow every character:</strong> spaces and all printable characters should be accepted, which makes passphrases practical, and pasting from password managers should be allowed.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Score Guide</h2>
      <div class="table-wrapper" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="score-guide-caption">
        <table class="table">
          <caption class="visually-hidden" id="score-guide-caption">What each zxcvbn score means</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Score</th><th scope="col">Guesses needed</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>0 Very weak</td><td>fewer than 10³</td><td>Among the first passwords any attacker tries</td></tr>
            <tr><td>1 Weak</td><td>10³ to 10⁶</td><td>Protects only against a heavily rate-limited online attack</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2 Fair</td><td>10⁶ to 10⁸</td><td>Survives an online attack but falls quickly offline</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3 Strong</td><td>10⁸ to 10¹⁰</td><td>Resists offline attacks on slow, salted hashes</td></tr>
            <tr><td>4 Very strong</td><td>more than 10¹⁰</td><td>Resists offline attacks even on fast hashes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The estimate cannot know whether a password has leaked in a data breach or is used on other sites, so a high score is necessary but not sufficient. Use a unique password for every account, turn on multi-factor authentication where you can, and let a password manager remember them.</p>
